me  the Southern Nevada Water Authority  wants other Western water districts just  to face reality and start conserving  precious resources in the face of what  is now a 20-year drought snwa feels it  is wasteful to grow certain water  intensive crops in parched desert  Landscapes but does the Water Authority  follow its own advice Chief 8 news now  investigator George Knapp has our story  I'll say in a in a word the answer is no  snwa is not following the advice that  it's handed out to other water agencies  in the west snwa has done a great job in  encouraging conservation of pressure  precious Water Resources by telling the  public to wise up and be water smart but  at the same time it's been pouring  precious water into a massive venture  California's Imperial Valley is a Bread  Basket where all manner of winter  vegetables are grown but a drive through  the valley makes it clear what grows  most of all field after field of thick  green Alfalfa it takes seven feet of  water to grow each acre of alfalfa all  of which is fed to animals and most of  which is exported to Asia especially to  China  this single District gets more Colorado  River water than the entire states of  Nevada and Arizona combined which is why  Nevada water officials have urged  changes in how water from the troubled  river is used well ordinarily I stay out  of my neighbor's business but when you  have a math problem and 80 percent of  the water use is in agriculture and 80  percent of that 80 percent is for forage  crops which are largely exported it's  hard not to see how that's not part of  the solution  Nevada ranchers and Farmers might want  to heed that advice water intensive  Alfalfa grown in the driest state in the  country is Nevada's second biggest  Agricultural Product after beef this  Ranch in White Pine County for example  is part of a seven property Empire that  stretches across more than 100 miles  north the South covering a vast 950 000  Acres overall who owns this sprawling  conglomerate you do the public the Great  Basin Ranch as it's known is owned and  operated by a public agency the Southern  Nevada Water Authority take a guess  what's the one and only crop grown on  snwa's vast ranching Empire in eastern  Nevada Alfalfa and a lot of it 8 600  tons of it last year why is a public  agency in the ranching business  competing directly against smaller  beleaguered Family Farms 15 years ago  snwa plunked down a whopping 79 million  dollars to buy seven ranches that at the  time were valued at one tenth that  amount the properties were intended as  beachheads for snwa's plan to build more  than 300 miles of pipes and pumps to  siphon billions of gallons of rural  groundwater and send it to Thirsty Las  Vegas cost estimates Grew From 1 billion  to 5 billion to 15 billion dollars  before snwa pulled it off the table the  plan was scrapped not because of the  expense but because opponents prevailed  in multiple Court battles arguing that  sucking an ocean of water from rural  aquifers would drain the water table and  create a huge dead zone  now years after the pipeline plan was  supposedly killed snwa is still pouring  public resources into its ranches this  New Mexico lawyer thinks it speaks  volumes but you think that the fact that  they still are pumping money and running  these ranches is a tell right what does  it tell us yeah I think it's a highly  suspicious activity there's no real  um  in any way reasonable explanation for  them to continuing to play the game of  ranch ownership and management up here  in White Pine County other than that  they are still somehow angling in the  future for a Toe Hold or a foothold to  allow them to take Rural Water  perskovitz was fighting cancer when he  prevailed over a battery of high-powered  lawyers employed by snwa records show  the agency has retained a long list of  expensive law firms to fight its various  battles the cows and sheep of the snwa  ranches have their very own lawyer  lobbyist who's paid six hundred thousand  dollars per year to represent them at  the Nevada legislature those legal costs  don't generally get included in the  financial statements of the Great Basin  Ranch statements which appear to show  the ranches operating in the black the  ranch spent Millions to buy all new  stock it spent Millions more on new  watering pivots to grow its Alfalfa an  impressive array of top-of-the-line  track actors and trucks new barns to  house all that hay unlike its smaller  neighbors this Ranch doesn't have to pay  a mortgage or property taxes or license  fees for its Vehicles boy if that's a  great neighbor I'm sure glad they  haven't been vindictive sheep Rancher  Hank Vogler has been in the crosshairs  of snwa ever since he came out opposed  to the pipeline plan although snwa vowed  to be a good neighbor when it bought the  seven ranches it's been embroiled in  multiple disputes with several of its  smaller Rivals expensive legal fights  over grazing rights that have drained  the wallets of family ranches already  strained by the drought records we  obtained from snwa show and ongoing  surveillance of Hank bogler and his  livestock the snwa ranch has filed  numerous complaints about bugler with  other government agencies that led to  investigations and more lawyer fees this  is what I spent my entire life trying to  do and now I'm trying to be shoved out  of business pushed aside destroyed by my  entire life savings is going down the  crapper  we wanted to ask snwa why they're using  public money to compete against private  businesses and why they're growing  Alfalfa but they declined to appear on  camera instead they responded to our  written questions we are posting those  on our website back to you

